window.docContents[181].resolve({"/Judgments-and-domains/Static-judgments/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Static-judgments":{"contents":"The judgment  states that the expression  has type  in the context . One rule per constructor of Expr.\n\nThe judgment  holds for the expressions that denote a location, a variable, *e, e.f, e->f and e[i].\n\nThe judgment  states that the command  is well typed and extends  to , so that a declaration reaches the following commands of the sequence. The return type  of the enclosing function is an implicit parameter.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Judgments and domains","header":"2.2. Static judgments","id":"/Judgments-and-domains/Static-judgments/#Core-C______--Judgments-and-domains--Static-judgments"},"/UD-I___-lexer-and-grammar/Abstract-syntax-and-parser/#Core-C______--UD-I___-lexer-and-grammar--Abstract-syntax-and-parser":{"contents":"The abstract syntax has one inductive type per class of nonterminals. Ty has the basic types, class, pointer and vector types and the internal type of nullptr. Expr has literals, nullptr, variable, unary and binary operators, conditional, call, the two forms of new, field access, arrow, dereference and indexing. Cmd has block, if, while, for, return, declaration with a type or with auto, assignment and expression statement. A Fun has a return type, a name, parameters and a body, a ClassDecl has a name and typed fields, and a Program is a list of declarations. The abstract grammar of the implemented subset follows, with one alternative per constructor of Syntax.lean and in the same order, the constructor named in the right column.Here  is an integer,  a boolean,  a variable identifier and  a function identifier. The abstract syntax has no parentheses and no precedence, because the tree fixes the structure.\n\nOne parser function per expression nonterminal, from Expr down to Primary. Each level of the grammar is one level of precedence, and the repetition ( op Operand )* builds a left associative tree. In the implemented subset PostfixExpr covers the function call, indexing, field access and arrow, UnaryExpr adds the dereference, and Primary covers literals, nullptr, variables, parenthesised expressions and the two forms of new. Method calls, this and lambdas are not parsed yet.\n\nOne parser function per command nonterminal. The function statement chooses the production by the first token, and exprStatement reads an expression and then decides between assignment and expression statement by the presence of =. In the implemented subset Type is a basic type, a class name optionally followed by *, or std::vector<Type> optionally followed by *. References come with UD III.\n\nA program is a sequence of declarations up to eof, each a class with public fields or a function. The function runParser runs the lexer and a parser on a string and requires the whole input to be consumed. Namespaces, templates, methods and constructors are not parsed yet.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009UD I, lexer and grammar","header":"1.3. Abstract syntax and parser","id":"/UD-I___-lexer-and-grammar/Abstract-syntax-and-parser/#Core-C______--UD-I___-lexer-and-grammar--Abstract-syntax-and-parser"}});